## 4. Small BV example (following Lemma 4.1)

Candidate addition:

> For two secret bits and one query, every recorded terminal vector has the form
> \(\Psi_s=c_\varnothing+s_1c_1+s_2c_2\).
> Therefore \(\Psi_{00}+\Psi_{10}+\Psi_{01}+\Psi_{11}=0\).
> Exact identification would place these four nonzero records in four distinct output-label groups, where they would be independent. The contradiction illustrates why extra ancillas and histories do not add new secret monomials.

## 5. Optional alternative BV proof

Candidate addition or replacement for the dimension-count conclusion:

> Fix a secret \(s^0\). Some coordinate in a terminal branch labelled \(s^0\) is nonzero at \(s^0\), because the full record is nonzero. That coordinate is a polynomial \(p(s)\) of degree at most \(q\), and exactness makes it zero at every other secret. Boolean multilinear interpolation gives
> \[
> p(s)=a\prod_{j:s^0_j=1}s_j\prod_{j:s^0_j=0}(1+s_j),\qquad a\ne0.
> \]
> Its top coefficient is nonzero, so its degree is \(n\), giving \(q\ge n\).

Keep the recorded-degree lemma and exactness assumptions. This is an alternative argument, not evidence of priority. A discussion of nondeterministic-polynomial methods should distinguish ordinary complex positive-acceptance algorithms from all-branches-correct modal identification.

