> There is no primitive vector \(\eta\in A^2\) satisfying \(X\eta=R\eta\), where primitive means that at least one coordinate is a unit. Indeed, \(X^2=I\) implies \(u^2\eta=0\), impossible for a unit coordinate. Nonprimitive eigenvectors can exist: \(\eta=(u^2,u^2+u^3)^T\) is nonzero and satisfies \(X\eta=R\eta\).

